{%- comment -%}
  Drawer shell. Line items are rendered client-side by theme.js from /cart.js,
  but the initial state is server-rendered from the real cart so a slow or
  failed script never shows "your cart is empty" to someone mid-purchase.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="cart-drawer-scrim" data-cart-scrim></div>

<aside class="cart-drawer"
       data-cart-drawer
       role="dialog"
       aria-modal="true"
       aria-labelledby="cart-drawer-title"
       aria-hidden="true">

  <div class="cart-drawer-head">
    <h2 id="cart-drawer-title">Your cart</h2>
    <button type="button" class="hdr-icon" data-cart-close aria-label="Close cart">
      {% render 'icon', name: 'close' %}
    </button>
  </div>

  <div class="cart-drawer-body" data-cart-body>
    {%- if cart.item_count == 0 -%}
      <div class="cart-empty">
        <p class="cart-empty-title">Your cart is empty</p>
        <p class="cart-empty-note">Free shipping and cash on delivery on every order.</p>
        <a class="cta cart-empty-cta" href="{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}">Start shopping</a>
      </div>
    {%- else -%}
      {%- for item in cart.items -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The quantity ceiling for this line. /cart.js carries no inventory at
          all, so the JS re-render has no way to know it — but Liquid does, and
          seeding it here means the + button stops offering a quantity the store
          cannot supply. `continue` means oversell is allowed, so the only cap
          is the theme's own 10; anything else is capped by real stock.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign line_max = 10
          if item.variant.inventory_management != blank and item.variant.inventory_policy != 'continue'
            assign line_max = item.variant.inventory_quantity
          endif
          if line_max > 10
            assign line_max = 10
          endif
          if line_max < 1
            assign line_max = 1
          endif
        -%}
        <div class="cart-line" data-line-key="{{ item.key }}" data-line-max="{{ line_max }}">
          <div class="cart-line-img">
            {%- comment -%}
              Through the shared snippet, like the cart page and every other
              image in the theme. Empty alt because the product title is right
              next to it — naming the image only makes a screen reader say the
              product twice. The presence check stays so .img-missing, which
              carries a 160px min-height for full-size plates, never lands in
              a 72px box.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if item.image -%}
              {% render 'responsive-image',
                 img: item.image,
                 alt: '',
                 widths: '72,144,216',
                 sizes: '72px',
                 base_width: 144,
                 loading: 'lazy' %}
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="cart-line-title">{{ item.product.title }}</p>
            {%- unless item.variant.title == 'Default Title' -%}
              <p class="cart-line-variant">{{ item.variant.title }}</p>
            {%- endunless -%}
            <p class="cart-line-price">{{ item.final_price | money }}</p>
            <p class="micro">Qty {{ item.quantity }}</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cart-line-total">{{ item.final_line_price | money }}</div>
        </div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="cart-drawer-foot" data-cart-foot {% if cart.item_count == 0 %}hidden{% endif %}>
    {%- comment -%}
      Label and value were the same size and weight, so "Subtotal" competed
      with the number a customer actually came here to read. The label is now
      secondary and the amount carries the weight.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      A rejected quantity change used to reach the shopper only through the
      visually-hidden live region, so a sighted customer who stepped past the
      available stock saw the number go up, the totals stay put, and no reason
      given. Same element and same role as the buy box's error line, placed
      above the subtotal it explains.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="cart-drawer-error" data-cart-error role="alert" hidden></p>

    <div class="cart-subtotal">
      <span class="cart-subtotal-label">Subtotal</span>
      <span class="cart-subtotal-value" data-cart-subtotal>{{ cart.total_price | money }}</span>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      One primary action, one secondary, one tertiary. Checkout and View cart
      were previously both full-width buttons of near-equal weight, which made
      the customer choose between two things that look equally like the way
      forward. View cart is a quieter text link now.

      The prepaid nudge already existed in the buy box and stopped there, so a
      customer who added from a card and opened the drawer never saw the offer
      at all — the one moment with the most intent behind it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a class="cta cta-block" href="/checkout">Checkout</a>

    {%- if settings.prepaid_discount_code != blank -%}
      <button type="button" class="cta-outline cta-block cart-prepaid"
              data-buy-prepaid data-discount-code="{{ settings.prepaid_discount_code }}">
        {{ settings.prepaid_discount_label | default: 'Save 5% paying online' }}
      </button>
    {%- endif -%}

    <a class="cart-view-link" href="{{ routes.cart_url }}">View cart</a>

    <p class="cart-drawer-note">Cash on delivery &middot; 30-day returns</p>
  </div>
</aside>

{% schema %}
{ "name": "Cart drawer" }
{% endschema %}
